[{:seon.task/name "with-merge-census-cost"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by session RUNNER (2026-10-21) sharding the census: `seon.check/census` now prints each checked contract's mg/check ms (100 runs, seed 42). Of the 82 checked contracts' ~130 s at load ~10, `seon.template/with-merge` alone takes 57 s (570 ms a run), then `seon.task/cycle-in` 15 s, `seon.proofs/failures` 11.7 s and `seon.proofs/costs` 11.2 s; the median contract is well under 1 s. with-merge (`seon.template/with-merge`) is one pass over its decls, so the work cannot cost 570 ms a call: the cost is in generating or checking `[:vector :seon.task/declaration]` (the generator's size, the armed contract validating every nested declaration on the way in and out, or task/task-id and task/default-branch's own contracts per element). Find which by measuring each part (generate only, validate only, call unarmed vs armed) and fix it at its cause: a schema whose generator is too large gets a bounded :gen, a contract check that repeats work does it once. One contract sets the floor of the sharded census (4 shards: 94 s wall, 57 s of it this contract); the census's whole run should be about its median contract times the count. Proof `seon.template-test/with-merge-checks-in-its-share`: a seon.cost/proof that with-merge plus its contract check grows linearly in the declarations given and costs no more per declaration than one task-id and one default-branch call (from seon.cost/units), measured at three sizes. Replaces nothing; deletes the cost."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/with-merge-checks-in-its-share}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-request-is-one-linked-transaction"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-22: \"Root should create the full set of tasks already linked (it's just one transaction with several maps) and only when everything is set should it fork and run it so they have the same data until that point.\" This is DESIGN-tasks D3(a): the template adds the merge task, in the same transaction.\n\nWhat exists, and the producer of each bad value:\n- A request has no template: `seon.template/with-merge` pairs declared work with a merge, and `seon.template/attach-tx` makes design -> implement -> test -> merge only for a failed attempt. A request never gets a design step.\n- Tasks are created held and released in a second transaction (`my.root/create!`, then `my.root/run!`; `seon.e2e` adds needs in a third).\n- `seon.turn/current-task` reads `[:turn :task]`, which only `seon.turn/work!` or a context read sets (`seon.turn/begin!`): the task rides `seon.root/spawn!`'s opts into root's view (`seon.root/start-agent`) and the writer's :spawn and fork messages drop it (`seon.writer/writer-proc`), so `my.task/current` is nil in an agent until its loop begins (an attached agent's first REPL read).\n- Each task of a chain gets its own default branch (`seon.task/default-branch`), so implement cannot see design's stubs (DESIGN-tasks §11 #13).\n- `seon.reconcile/done-refusals` counts a proof with no result as :proof-red (`(get results p :none)`); v1 counted it unknown.\n\nThe change:\n- `seon.template/request`: a declaration (the request: ns, name, plan, optional proofs and model) becomes design -> implement -> merge, all on design's branch, linked by :needs; the merge map is the one `seon.template/with-merge` makes.\n- `my.root/request!`: `seon.task/create-tx` and `seon.task/execution-tx` in one `seon.root/transact-main!`. The reconciler forks only from a committed main, so every agent of the set starts from the same graph.\n- The task rides the writer's :spawn and fork messages to `seon.agent/init!`, which keeps it in the agent's state; `seon.turn/current-task` reads it; `[:turn :task]` is deleted.\n- An unrun proof refuses done as :proof-unknown, not :proof-red.\n\nA failed task is held for root (owner, 2026-10-22: \"We need a way to mark the task as failed and root should be notified ... where we are just updating the task data and root is monitoring for these situations\"; D2(a)). Before: my.task/send-back! (deleted) was a turn stop carrying a string, outcome :sent-back, which the judge read as a failed attempt and refined (`seon.template/attach-tx`): an automatic retry. Agents commit their own branches, so the writer never sees a branch's transactions (`seon.writer/writer-proc` gets only :agent-commit); the event root already reacts to is the attempt's end, when `seon.reconcile/judge-tx` reads the branch. Now `my.task/fail!` writes `:seon.task/failure` {:reason :counterexample :attempt} on the task on its branch and ends the loop (outcome :failed); the judge carries a failure of this attempt (the attempt rides the fork, as the task does) onto main with the task held; the existing judged rule closes the attempt; `seon.reconcile/task-state` derives :failed (held with a failure); `seon.problems/report` has a :failed class. A release (`my.root/run!`) is root's answer; the failure stays as a record. send-back! is deleted. No proc, poll or timer is added.\n\nTask content reaches main at the attempt's end (owner, 2026-10-22, D4; root chose the judge's carry). Before: `seon.reconcile/judge-tx` carried only the attempt's own plan, without comparing it to the base, and a merge carries no task data (`:data-attrs #{}`). Now `seon.task/carry-tx` compares base, branch and main over `seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs`: a branch-only change overwrites main's, and a two-sided different edit is recorded on the attempt (`:seon.attempt/task-conflicts`, the `:task-conflict` class of `seon.problems/report`) for root. The writer's :branch-db read answers the merge-base db. seon.task/plan-tx is deleted.\n\nD5(a) (owner, 2026-10-22): a task's own checks live with the task, outside main's test namespaces; this worktree session adds none, so main's test tier is unchanged.\n\nNot decided here (root's): proofs still accumulate along :needs (§11 #14), and a design step's new proofs reach main only as the plan's text (§11 #11, #16), so an implement step whose request carried no proofs is refused :no-proofs by root's judge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/a-request-is-design-implement-merge-on-one-branch
                      seon.template-test/a-request-is-one-transaction-and-forks-after-it
                      seon.reconcile-test/done-refusals-table
                      seon.agent-test/a-forked-agent-knows-its-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-task-marked-failed-is-held-for-root
                      seon.agent-test/a-failure-marked-through-the-server-holds-the-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-branch-carries-every-task-it-changed-and-records-a-two-sided-edit}}]
